  <!-- SECTION 2: HETEROTIC GROUPS & DIVERSITY -->
  <section id="amaizing-diversity" aria-labelledby="amaizing-diversity-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="amaizing-diversity-title">Heterotic groups</h2></div>
    </div>

<div class="amaizing-diversity-grid">
      <article class="amaizing-diversity-card">
        <h3>European Flint &amp; Dent Heterotic Complementarity</h3>
        <p>
          European hybrid maize production relies on the complementary heterotic grouping between European Flint \(represented by F2 and F4\) and Dent/Iodent lines \(MBS847, F252\). The AMAIZING reference genomes provide the first comprehensive, chromosome-scale pan-genomic atlas dissecting structural variants and non-shared gene content between these pools.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="amaizing-diversity-card">
        <h3>Early Flowering &amp; North American Introgression</h3>
        <p>
          Lines such as Gaspe1_1_1 \(GF111\) and F252 incorporate extremely early flowering alleles that enabled maize cultivation to expand northward into temperate Europe and Canada. High-continuity sequence assemblies resolve complex promoter rearrangements and copy number variants at major flowering time loci.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="amaizing-diversity-card">
        <h3>Multi-Tissue Transcriptomics &amp; Water-Deficit Response</h3>
        <p>
          Each genome assembly is accompanied by triplicate RNA-seq profiling across 15 distinct developmental tissues, including comparative physiological assays under well-watered and controlled water-deficit regimes, mapping gene regulatory networks governing drought resilience.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: KEY PUBLICATIONS & ABSTRACT PREVIEWS -->
  <section id="amaizing-publications" aria-labelledby="amaizing-pub-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
