      $(reference_cards)
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: DATA HOSTED AT MAIZEGDB -->
  <section id="amaizing-data-hosted" aria-labelledby="amaizing-hosted-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="amaizing-hosted-title">Hosted data</h2></div>
    </div>

<div class="amaizing-hosted-grid">
      <!-- 1: JBrowse 2 -->
      <article class="amaizing-hosted-card">
        <h3>Genome Browsers \(JBrowse 2\)</h3>
        <p>Interactive chromosome-scale browsers for all 7 AMAIZING assemblies featuring gene models, panEDTA repeat tracks, and synteny views.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="/genomebrowser">Open Genome Browsers &#8599;</a>
      </article>

      <!-- 2: Gene Center -->
      <article class="amaizing-hosted-card">
        <h3>Gene Center &amp; Models</h3>
        <p>High-confidence structural and functional gene annotations across all 7 lines indexed in the Gene Center with GO terms.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="/gene_center/gene">Explore Gene Center &#8599;</a>
      </article>

      <!-- 3: BLAST -->
      <article class="amaizing-hosted-card">
        <h3>Sequence BLAST Tool</h3>
        <p>Align nucleotide, CDS, transcript, and translated protein queries directly against AMAIZING assemblies and gene models.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="$(blast_url)">Run BLAST Search &#8599;</a>
      </article>

      <!-- 4: Bulk Downloads -->
      <article class="amaizing-hosted-card">
        <h3>Bulk Downloads &amp; Globus</h3>
        <p>Download complete assembly FASTA files, GFF3 annotations, canonical CDS/PEP sequences, and panEDTA TE tracks.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="/download#downloads-amaizing">Browse Downloads &#8599;</a>
      </article>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5: SCALE & GENOMIC METRICS DASHBOARD -->
  <section id="amaizing-faqs" aria-labelledby="amaizing-faqs-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="amaizing-faqs-title">FAQs</h2></div>
    </div>

<div class="amaizing-faq-list">
      <details class="amaizing-faq-item">
        <summary><h3>What is the AMAIZING project and which institutions contributed?</h3></summary>
        <div class="amaizing-faq-body">
          <p>
            The AMAIZING project is a major French national public-private collaborative program supported by the French National Research Agency \(ANR\), led by INRAE in partnership with CEA-Genoscope, Arvalis Institut du V&eacute;g&eacute;tal, CIRAD, CNRS, private plant breeding companies, and international databases including MaizeGDB. Learn more at <a href="https://amaizing.fr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">amaizing.fr</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="amaizing-faq-item">
        <summary><h3>Where can I download the complete FASTA and GFF3 files for AMAIZING lines?</h3></summary>
        <div class="amaizing-faq-body">
          <p>
            Full-genome FASTA sequences, GFF3 gene annotations, CDS/PEP files, and panEDTA transposable element tracks can be downloaded directly from the <a href="/download#downloads-amaizing">MaizeGDB Downloads Portal</a> or via individual assembly download links in the table above.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="amaizing-faq-item">
        <summary><h3>How do AMAIZING gene models compare with B73 RefGen_v5?</h3></summary>
        <div class="amaizing-faq-body">
          <p>
            The AMAIZING genomes capture significant pan-genomic structural variation, including thousands of non-syntenic genes, presence-absence variants, and TE insertions that are absent from B73. Comparative tracks and synteny views are viewable directly in <a href="/genomebrowser">JBrowse 2</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="amaizing-faq-item">
        <summary><h3>Can I search these genomes using BLAST at MaizeGDB?</h3></summary>
        <div class="amaizing-faq-body">
          <p>
            Yes! MaizeGDB maintains dedicated BLAST databases for all seven AMAIZING lines. Go to the <a href="$(blast_url)">BLAST Tool</a> and choose the desired AMAIZING assembly or predicted peptide database.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 7: CONNECTED RESOURCES -->
